=begin

    Moves combat messaging out of your main story window and into its own "CombatStream" stream, so a
    frontend window set up for that stream shows the fight and the story window stays readable.

    Combat lines are recognized with Lich's own Gemstone combat definitions (the same ones the
    combat tracker uses): attacks, AS/DS and CS/TD rolls, damage, crits, flares, evades/blocks/
    parries, status effects and spell wear-offs. Nothing is squelched and no other script is
    affected -- scripts still see every line exactly as the game sent it.

    USAGE:
        ;combatstream                       - start routing combat, using previous settings

    OPTIONAL PARAMETERS:
        --block                             - (default) once a combat line is seen, everything up to the next
                                              prompt goes to the combat stream too (catches death messages,
                                              crit flavor and roundtime that follow an attack)
        --line                              - only move the individual lines Lich recognizes as combat
        --stream=<id>                       - stream id (and window title) to send combat to (default: CombatStream)
        --no-mirror                         - don't copy the commands and lines since the last prompt (usually
                                              the attack itself, when Lich doesn't recognize it) into the
                                              combat stream
        --mirror                            - (default) turn that copy back on
        --compress                          - show one summary line per creature per round instead of the combat
                                              text itself (damage, hits, crits, flares, kills, HP estimate), using
                                              Lich's combat tracker. Needs combat tracking on and a Lich newer than
                                              5.21.0; falls back to the full text for any round it can't summarize
        --no-compress                       - (default) show the combat text itself

        --debug                             - echo which kind of combat each moved line was, for this run only
        --help                              - show this help and exit
        --list                              - show current settings and exit

    FRONTEND SETUP:
        Wrayth/Stormfront, Genie, etc.      - a window titled with the stream id is created (streamWindow/exposeStream) at startup
        ProfanityFE                         - add a window to your layout whose value includes the stream id
        Wizard/Avalon                       - not supported (no stream windows)

        If your frontend has no window for the stream, it may drop the text rather than show it in the
        main window -- open the window first. The default id is not "combat": the game, ;targetlist and
        Saga already use that stream.

        author: elanthia-online
  contributors: Tysong
          game: Gemstone
          name: combatstream
          tags: combat, stream, window, frontend
      required: Lich >= 5.21.0
       version: 1.0.0

    changelog:
      1.0.0 (2026-09-22)
        - Initial release

=end

module CombatStream
  # Recognizes combat lines with lich-5's Gemstone combat definitions.
  #
  # Each family is called directly and synchronously. Combat::Tracker is not
  # used: its hook only observes, and it parses after the prompt on a worker
  # thread, so its events arrive after the line has already reached the
  # frontend. The def tables themselves are plain, gated pattern lookups that
  # need neither the tracker enabled nor its settings loaded.
  #
  # Built from whatever the running lich-5 actually provides, so a release
  # that lacks a family (e.g. Definitions::Messages, newer than 5.21) just
  # classifies without it instead of failing to start.
  module Classifier
    # [family, constant path under Lich::Gemstone::Combat, method], cheapest
    # and most frequent first -- the first family to match wins.
    CANDIDATES = [
      [:resolution, 'Definitions::Resolutions', :parse],
      [:damage,     'Definitions::Damage',      :parse],
      [:attack,     'Parser',                   :parse_attack],
      [:outcome,    'Definitions::Outcomes',    :parse],
      [:flare,      'Definitions::Flares',      :parse],
      [:status,     'Definitions::Statuses',    :parse],
      [:ucs,        'Definitions::UCS',         :parse],
      [:spell_loss, 'Definitions::SpellLosses', :parse],
      [:sequence,   'Definitions::Sequences',   :parse_start],
      [:sequence,   'Definitions::Sequences',   :parse_end],
      [:assault,    'Definitions::Assaults',    :parse_start],
      [:assault,    'Definitions::Assaults',    :parse_end],
      [:crit_rider, 'Definitions::Resolutions', :crit_rider_line?]
    ].freeze

    # @param gemstone [Module] the Lich::Gemstone namespace to read defs from
    # @return [Array<Array(Symbol, #call)>] the available [family, matcher] pairs
    def self.build(gemstone = Lich::Gemstone)
      combat = gemstone.const_get(:Combat, false)
      families = CANDIDATES.filter_map do |family, path, meth|
        mod = lookup(combat, path)
        [family, mod.method(meth)] if mod.respond_to?(meth)
      end

      # Scattered combat messages (disarms, ambushes, rooting ...); only in
      # lich-5 newer than 5.21.
      messages = lookup(combat, 'Definitions::Messages')
      families << [:message, ->(line) { messages.scan(line).any? }] if messages.respond_to?(:scan)

      # Crit description text ("Torn muscle in ...'s left leg!"). Returns a
      # Hash of matches, empty when none.
      crits = lookup(gemstone, 'CritRanks')
      families << [:crit, ->(line) { crits.parse(line).any? }] if crits.respond_to?(:parse)

      families
    end

    # @return [Module, nil] the constant at path under root, nil if absent
    def self.lookup(root, path)
      path.split('::').reduce(root) do |ns, name|
        ns&.const_defined?(name, false) ? ns.const_get(name, false) : nil
      end
    end

    # @return [Symbol, nil] the first family that recognizes line, or nil
    def self.classify(families, line)
      families.each do |family, matcher|
        return family if matcher.call(line)
      end
      nil
    end
  end

  # The DownstreamHook body: decides, one server string at a time, whether
  # it goes to the combat stream.
  #
  # Only what the frontend sees changes. DownstreamHooks run after Lich has
  # parsed the line and fed it to every script's buffer, so waitfor/matchwait/
  # Flags and the combat tracker still see the original text.
  class Router
    PROMPT = '<prompt'
    # Not "combat": GemStone's own stream, ;targetlist and Saga all use that id.
    DEFAULT_STREAM = 'CombatStream'
    PUSH_STREAM = /<pushStream\b/.freeze
    POP_STREAM = /<popStream\b/.freeze

    # Strings carrying frontend structure (room, stream, dialog or mono
    # state) are never wrapped -- the structure would move with them -- and
    # they end any combat block, since combat does not span a room change.
    # The last alternative catches a tag split across two strings.
    STRUCTURAL = %r{</?(?:clearStream|streamWindow|exposeStream|compDef|compass|component|dialogData|openDialog|nav|app|mode|style|output|resource|playerID|settingsInfo|indicator|progressBar|spell|left|right)\b|<[^>]*\z}.freeze

    # Conversation stays in the story window even when it lands mid-fight.
    CONVERSATION = /<preset id=['"](?:speech|whisper|thought)['"]/.freeze

    attr_reader :stream, :mode

    # @param classifier [#call] line -> family Symbol or nil
    # @param stream [String] stream id to route combat into
    # @param mode [Symbol] :block or :line (see the script help)
    # @param mirror [Boolean] copy the story lines since the last prompt
    #   into the stream when a combat block starts (see #call)
    # @param commands [#call, nil] returns the command log, oldest first
    #   ($_CLIENTBUFFER_ in the script); nil leaves commands out of the mirror
    # @param compress [#call, nil] compress mode: routed lines are held
    #   instead of wrapped, and at each prompt this is called with
    #   (prompt_time, commands, raw_lines) for the round (see Compressor)
    # @param debug [#call, nil] called with (family, line) for each routed line
    def initialize(classifier, stream: DEFAULT_STREAM, mode: :block, mirror: true, commands: nil, compress: nil,
                   debug: nil)
      @classifier = classifier
      @stream = stream
      @mode = mode
      @mirror = mirror
      @commands = commands
      @compress = compress
      @debug = debug
      @in_block = false
      @stream_depth = 0
      @pending = []
      @command_mark = latest_command
      @round_commands = []
      @round_raw = []
    end

    # Most lines held back for mirroring, in case no prompt comes for a
    # long stretch.
    MIRROR_LIMIT = 20

    # @param line [String] one raw server string
    # @return [String, nil] the string, wrapped in the combat stream or
    #   unchanged; nil in compress mode for a line held for the round summary
    def call(line)
      return line unless line.is_a?(String)

      family = route(line)
      return line unless family

      @in_block = true if @mode == :block
      # The lines leading into a fight (the attack itself when it is "You
      # gesture at ..." or an odd weapon verb, a creature's lunge ...) often
      # match no def, so the block starts on the roll or damage line after
      # them. They have already gone to the story window and can't be taken
      # back, but copies can open the block so the combat window shows the
      # whole exchange since the last prompt.
      commands, held = @mirror && family != :block ? lead_in : [[], []]
      @pending.clear
      @debug&.call(family, line)

      if @compress
        # The summary replaces the round's lines; they are kept only so the
        # round can be shown as-is if no summary comes.
        @round_commands.concat(commands)
        @round_raw.concat(held) << line
        return nil
      end

      out = +''
      (commands + held).each { |text| out << wrap(text) }
      out << wrap(line)
    end

    # @return [Array(Array<String>, Array<String>)] the commands sent and
    #   the story lines held since the last prompt
    def lead_in
      commands = new_commands
      commands.each { |command| @debug&.call(:command, command) }
      @pending.each { |held| @debug&.call(:mirror, held) }
      [commands, @pending.dup]
    end

    # @return [Symbol, nil] why line goes to the stream (a def family, or
    #   :block inside a combat block), or nil to leave it in place
    def route(line)
      if line.include?(PROMPT)
        finish_round(line) if @compress
        # Lich core closes a server stream left open at the prompt, so ours
        # resets here too rather than getting stuck.
        @in_block = false
        @stream_depth = 0
        # Commands already in the log belong to this prompt's output, so
        # only ones sent after it count as leading into the next fight.
        @command_mark = latest_command
        return clear_pending
      end

      # Track streams the server opens itself (its own combat, thoughts,
      # familiar ...). Lines inside one already go to a window; wrapping them
      # again would nest streams.
      if PUSH_STREAM.match?(line) || POP_STREAM.match?(line)
        @stream_depth = [@stream_depth + line.scan(PUSH_STREAM).size - line.scan(POP_STREAM).size, 0].max
        return nil
      end
      # Already in a window of its own: never routed or mirrored, but it
      # doesn't break up the story lines around it either.
      return nil if @stream_depth.positive?

      if STRUCTURAL.match?(line)
        # A room change or the like: what came before is another scene.
        @in_block = false
        return clear_pending
      end
      # Speech stays out of both the stream and the mirror, without losing
      # the fight lines around it.
      return nil if CONVERSATION.match?(line)

      return :block if @in_block
      return nil if line.strip.empty?

      family = @classifier.call(line)
      # Plain story text that stays put is held for mirroring if a combat
      # line follows it before the next prompt.
      unless family
        @pending << line
        @pending.shift if @pending.size > MIRROR_LIMIT
      end
      family
    end

    PROMPT_TIME = /<prompt time=["'](\d+)/.freeze

    # Hands the round's held lines to the compressor, stamped with the
    # prompt's server time -- the same time Combat::Tracker stamps on the
    # attack events it parses from this chunk, which is how they meet up.
    def finish_round(prompt)
      return if @round_raw.empty?

      @compress.call(prompt[PROMPT_TIME, 1]&.to_i, @round_commands, @round_raw)
      @round_commands = []
      @round_raw = []
    end

    # Forget the held lines; returns nil so route can return it.
    def clear_pending
      @pending.clear
      nil
    end

    # Commands sent since the mark, as the story window shows them
    # ("[bigshot]>ambush ..." for a script, ">look" for one you typed),
    # then moves the mark past them.
    #
    # The log holds a script's command as "[name]>" + $cmd_prefix + command
    # and a typed one as $cmd_prefix + command. Its entries are found by
    # identity, not text, so the same command sent twice still counts as
    # new. If the mark has aged out of the log (bounded), everything left
    # is newer than it.
    def new_commands
      log = command_log
      return [] if log.empty?

      mark = log.rindex { |entry| entry.equal?(@command_mark) }
      fresh = mark ? log[(mark + 1)..] : log
      @command_mark = log.last
      fresh.last(MIRROR_LIMIT).map { |entry| display_command(entry) }
    end

    COMMAND_PREFIX = /\A(\[[^\]]*\]>)?(?:<c>)?/.freeze

    def display_command(entry)
      text = entry.to_s.chomp.sub(COMMAND_PREFIX) { Regexp.last_match(1) || '>' }
      # Our own XML is wrapped around it, so the command itself must not be
      # read as markup ("say <grin>").
      text.gsub('&', '&amp;').gsub('<', '&lt;')
    end

    def latest_command
      command_log.last
    end

    def command_log
      @commands&.call || []
    end

    # Each line is its own push/pop pair rather than one stream held open
    # across strings: a lost prompt can never leave the story window stuck
    # inside our stream, and bold/mono tags stay balanced within the pair.
    def wrap(line)
      "<pushStream id=\"#{@stream}\"/>#{line.chomp}\r\n<popStream/>\r\n"
    end
  end

  # Turns one round's Combat::Tracker attack events into one line per
  # creature (and one for you), totalling every swing and flare that landed
  # on it:
  #
  #   greater construct: 20 dmg, 1 hit, L leg r1, ensorcell [~64% hp]
  #   you: 0 dmg (1 evaded)
  module Summary
    OUTCOME_WORDS = {
      evade: 'evaded', block: 'blocked', parry: 'parried', miss: 'missed', warded: 'warded',
      fumble: 'fumbled', hindrance: 'hindered', confused: 'confused'
    }.freeze

    # @param events [Array<Hash>] combat.attack payloads, in order
    # @param hp [#call, nil] creature id -> estimated HP percent, or nil
    #   when unknown
    # @return [Array<String>] one line per creature, first seen first
    def self.lines(events, hp: nil)
      groups = {}
      events.each { |event| add_event(groups, event) }
      groups.each_value.filter_map { |group| summary_line(group, hp) }
    end

    def self.add_event(groups, event)
      target = event[:target] || {}
      attacker = event[:attacker] || {}
      main = if event[:inbound]
               group(groups, :you, 'you')
             elsif event[:foreign_caster]
               # Another player's attack: shown, but kept apart from ours.
               group(groups, [:foreign, attacker[:id] || attacker[:name], target[:id] || target[:name]],
                     "#{attacker[:name]} on #{target[:name] || 'something'}")
             else
               creature_group(groups, target)
             end

      if main
        (event[:hits] || []).each { |hit| add_hit(main, hit, count: true) }
        (event[:outcomes] || []).each { |outcome| main[:outcomes][outcome] += 1 }
        (event[:statuses] || []).each { |status| main[:statuses] << status.to_s }
      end

      (event[:flares] || []).each do |flare|
        # A flare can land on another creature than the swing (an AoE, or a
        # reactive flare on an inbound attack hitting its attacker).
        owner = creature_group(groups, flare[:target_info]) if flare[:target_info]
        owner ||= event[:inbound] ? creature_group(groups, attacker) : main
        next unless owner

        owner[:flares] << flare[:name].to_s.tr('_', ' ')
        (flare[:hits] || []).each { |hit| add_hit(owner, hit, count: false) }
        (flare[:outcomes] || []).each { |outcome| owner[:outcomes][outcome] += 1 }
      end
    end

    def self.creature_group(groups, who)
      return nil unless who && (who[:id] || who[:name])

      group(groups, [:creature, who[:id] || who[:name]], who[:name] || "##{who[:id]}", id: who[:id])
    end

    def self.group(groups, key, label, id: nil)
      groups[key] ||= { label: label, id: id, damage: 0, hits: 0, crits: [], flares: [], statuses: [],
                        outcomes: Hash.new(0), killed: false }
    end

    # A flare's hits add damage and crits but aren't counted as swings.
    def self.add_hit(group, hit, count:)
      group[:damage] += hit[:damage].to_i
      group[:hits] += 1 if count
      crit = hit[:crit]
      return unless crit

      group[:crits] << crit_text(crit)
      group[:statuses] << 'stunned' if crit[:stunned].to_i.positive?
      group[:killed] ||= crit[:fatal]
    end

    def self.crit_text(crit)
      return 'coup de grace' if crit[:type] == 'coup_de_grace'

      location = crit[:location].to_s.sub(/\Aleft /, 'L ').sub(/\Aright /, 'R ')
      crit[:rank] ? "#{location} r#{crit[:rank]}" : location
    end

    def self.summary_line(group, hp)
      outcomes = group[:outcomes].map { |outcome, n| "#{n} #{OUTCOME_WORDS.fetch(outcome, outcome.to_s)}" }
      # Nothing landed and nothing was avoided: not worth a line.
      return nil if group[:damage].zero? && group[:hits].zero? && group[:crits].empty? &&
                    group[:flares].empty? && group[:statuses].empty? && outcomes.empty? && !group[:killed]

      parts = ["#{group[:damage]} dmg"]
      parts << "#{group[:hits]} hit#{'s' unless group[:hits] == 1}" if group[:hits].positive?
      parts.concat(group[:crits])
      parts.concat(group[:flares].uniq)
      parts.concat(group[:statuses].uniq)
      text = "#{group[:label]}: #{parts.join(', ')}"
      text << " (#{outcomes.join(', ')})" unless outcomes.empty?
      if group[:killed]
        text << ' -- killed'
      elsif group[:id] && hp && (percent = hp.call(group[:id]))
        text << " [~#{percent}% hp]"
      end
      text
    end

    private_class_method :add_event, :creature_group, :group, :add_hit, :crit_text, :summary_line
  end

  # Pairs each round the Router held back with the attack events
  # Combat::Tracker parsed from the same chunk, and decides what to show.
  #
  # The tracker parses each chunk on its own worker thread after the prompt,
  # so the two arrive separately and in either order. They meet on the
  # prompt's server time, which the tracker stamps on every event (:at; see
  # round_for for the one-second slack), and
  # a batch is complete once all observation_batch[:size] of its events are
  # in. A round whose batch never comes (tracking off, nothing the tracker
  # could parse, a lagging worker) is shown as-is after ROUND_WAIT: text is
  # never silently lost.
  #
  # No threads or Lich calls here -- the script feeds it and prints what it
  # returns.
  class Compressor
    ROUND_WAIT = 1.5
    # A complete batch with no round (a chunk we routed nothing from) is
    # kept this long in case its round is still on the way.
    BATCH_KEEP = 5.0

    # @param summarize [#call] events -> Array<String> summary lines
    def initialize(summarize)
      @summarize = summarize
      @rounds = []
      @batches = {}
    end

    # @return [Array<Array<String>>] outputs ready to show, each a list of
    #   lines (commands, then the summary or the raw lines)
    def add_round(time, commands, raw, now:)
      @rounds << { time: time, commands: commands, raw: raw, deadline: now + ROUND_WAIT }
      settle(now)
    end

    # @param event [Hash] a combat.attack payload
    def add_event(event, now:)
      batch = event[:observation_batch]
      return settle(now) unless batch

      entry = (@batches[batch[:id]] ||= { time: event[:at]&.to_i, size: batch[:size], events: [], seen: now })
      entry[:events] << event
      settle(now)
    end

    # Call periodically so rounds without a batch are released.
    def tick(now:)
      settle(now)
    end

    # @return [Boolean] true while anything is waiting
    def waiting?
      !@rounds.empty? || !@batches.empty?
    end

    private

    def settle(now)
      out = []
      @batches.keys.sort.each do |id|
        batch = @batches[id]
        next if batch[:events].size < batch[:size]

        if (index = round_for(batch[:time]))
          # Batches arrive in chunk order, so any earlier round still
          # waiting will never get one.
          @rounds.shift(index).each { |round| out << raw_output(round) }
          out << summary_output(@rounds.shift, batch[:events])
          @batches.delete(id)
        elsif now - batch[:seen] > BATCH_KEEP
          @batches.delete(id)
        end
      end
      out << raw_output(@rounds.shift) while @rounds.first && @rounds.first[:deadline] <= now
      out
    end

    # The round stamped with this batch's time: exact first, else one second
    # either way. Both sides shift the prompt's server time by
    # XMLData.server_time_offset, which is recomputed at every prompt; the
    # tracker's worker can read it a prompt later than the hook did, and
    # since server time is whole seconds the two can floor one apart.
    def round_for(time)
      return nil unless time

      @rounds.index { |round| round[:time] == time } ||
        @rounds.index { |round| round[:time] && (round[:time] - time).abs <= 1 }
    end

    def summary_output(round, events)
      summary = @summarize.call(events)
      return raw_output(round) if summary.empty?

      round[:commands] + summary
    end

    def raw_output(round)
      round[:commands] + round[:raw]
    end
  end

  HOOK_NAME = 'combatstream'
  STREAM_ID_PATTERN = /\A[a-zA-Z][\w-]*\z/.freeze

  # Hooks chain: each one gets the previous hook's output. Ours wraps lines
  # in stream tags, so it must run LAST, after Combat::Tracker's hook and
  # any other script's, or they would parse our wrapped text instead of the
  # game's (and anchored patterns stop matching). Equal priorities run in
  # registration order, and the tracker re-registers whenever it is turned
  # on, so only an explicit low priority keeps ours last. Lich 5.21 has no
  # priorities, so the option is passed only where add accepts it.
  HOOK_PRIORITY = -1_000

  # @return [Hash] the keyword options this lich-5's hook registry accepts
  def self.hook_options(registry)
    accepted = registry.method(:add).parameters.map(&:last)
    options = {}
    options[:persist] = false if accepted.include?(:persist)
    options[:priority] = HOOK_PRIORITY if accepted.include?(:priority)
    options
  end

  def self.show_help
    respond <<~HELP
      combatstream - moves combat messaging into its own stream/window

        ;combatstream                 start, using previous settings
        ;combatstream --block         (default) route everything from a combat line to the next prompt
        ;combatstream --line          route only lines recognized as combat
        ;combatstream --stream=<id>   stream id and window title to use (default: CombatStream)
        ;combatstream --no-mirror     don't copy the commands and lines since the last prompt into the stream
        ;combatstream --mirror        (default) copy them when combat starts; they stay in the main window too
        ;combatstream --compress      one summary line per creature per round (needs combat tracking on)
        ;combatstream --no-compress   (default) the combat text itself
        ;combatstream --debug         echo the kind of each routed line (this run only)
        ;combatstream --list          show settings
        ;combatstream --help          show this help

      A window titled with the stream id is created at startup (Wrayth, Genie ...). ProfanityFE needs
      a window in its layout for the stream id.
    HELP
  end

  def self.show_settings
    respond "combatstream settings:"
    respond "  mode:   #{CharSettings['mode'] || 'block'}"
    respond "  mirror: #{CharSettings['mirror'] != false}"
    respond "  compress: #{CharSettings['compress'] == true}"
    respond "  stream: #{CharSettings['stream'] || Router::DEFAULT_STREAM}"
  end

  def self.run(args)
    if args.include?('--help') || args.include?('-h')
      show_help
      return
    end

    unless XMLData.game =~ /^GS/ && defined?(Lich::Gemstone::Combat::Parser)
      echo 'combatstream needs Gemstone IV and Lich 5.21.0 or newer (the Gemstone combat definitions).'
      return
    end

    if Frontend.respond_to?(:supports_streams?) && !Frontend.supports_streams?
      echo "Your frontend (#{Frontend.client}) has no stream windows, so there is nowhere to move combat to."
      return
    end

    CharSettings['mode'] = 'block' if args.include?('--block')
    CharSettings['mode'] = 'line' if args.include?('--line')
    CharSettings['mirror'] = true if args.include?('--mirror')
    CharSettings['mirror'] = false if args.include?('--no-mirror')
    CharSettings['compress'] = true if args.include?('--compress')
    CharSettings['compress'] = false if args.include?('--no-compress')
    if (stream_arg = args.find { |a| a.start_with?('--stream=') })
      stream = stream_arg.split('=', 2).last
      unless STREAM_ID_PATTERN.match?(stream)
        echo "Invalid stream id #{stream.inspect}: use letters, digits, - or _, starting with a letter."
        return
      end
      CharSettings['stream'] = stream
    end

    if args.include?('--list')
      show_settings
      return
    end

    stream = CharSettings['stream'] || Router::DEFAULT_STREAM
    mode = CharSettings['mode'] == 'line' ? :line : :block
    families = Classifier.build
    debug = ->(family, line) { respond "[combatstream] #{family}: #{line.gsub(/<[^>]+>/, '').strip}" } if args.include?('--debug')

    # Create and show the stream's window before anything is routed into it.
    # Sent on every stream-capable frontend, as DRC.log_window does:
    # frontends that don't know these tags ignore them. Titled with the id
    # itself, so the window to look for is the one named after --stream.
    _respond("<streamWindow id=\"#{stream}\" title=\"#{stream}\" location=\"center\" save=\"true\" />")
    _respond("<exposeStream id=\"#{stream}\"/>")

    classifier = ->(line) { Classifier.classify(families, line) }
    # A snapshot ($_CLIENTBUFFER_ is appended to from script threads).
    commands = -> { $_CLIENTBUFFER_.dup.to_a }

    compress = CharSettings['compress'] == true
    if compress && (problem = compress_problem)
      echo "--compress is off for this run: #{problem}"
      compress = false
    end
    # Rounds from the hook and attack events from the tracker's worker both
    # land here; only this script's own thread touches the Compressor.
    queue = Thread::Queue.new if compress
    # The tracker stamps events with the prompt's time shifted by the
    # server clock offset (Processor.prompt_time), so the round's time is
    # shifted the same way for the two to meet.
    if compress
      on_round = lambda do |time, round_commands, raw|
        time = (time + XMLData.server_time_offset.to_f).floor if time
        queue << [:round, time, round_commands, raw]
      end
    end

    router = Router.new(classifier, stream: stream, mode: mode, mirror: CharSettings['mirror'] != false,
                                    commands: commands, compress: on_round, debug: debug)
    warned = false
    hook = proc do |server_string|
      router.call(server_string)
    rescue StandardError => e
      # DownstreamHook removes a hook that raises. One odd line must not
      # silently turn routing off for the rest of the session.
      unless warned
        warned = true
        Lich.log "combatstream: #{e.class}: #{e.message}\n\t#{e.backtrace&.first(5)&.join("\n\t")}"
      end
      server_string
    end

    if compress
      tracker = Lich::Gemstone::Combat::Tracker
      # Tracker callbacks run on its worker thread and must stay cheap:
      # just queue the event for this script's thread.
      tracker.on(:attack, name: HOOK_NAME) { |_topic, event| queue << [:event, event] }
      before_dying { tracker.off(HOOK_NAME) }
    end

    before_dying { DownstreamHook.remove(HOOK_NAME) }
    DownstreamHook.add(HOOK_NAME, hook, **hook_options(DownstreamHook))
    respond "[combatstream] routing combat to stream '#{stream}' (#{mode} mode#{', compressed' if compress}, " \
            "#{families.size} combat families). ;kill combatstream to stop."
    compress ? run_compressor(queue, router) : sleep
  end

  # @return [String, nil] why --compress can't run here, or nil if it can
  def self.compress_problem
    combat = Lich::Gemstone::Combat
    unless defined?(Lich::Common::Events) && combat::Processor.respond_to?(:attack_events_requested?)
      return 'it needs a Lich newer than 5.21.0 (Combat::Tracker attack events).'
    end
    # Held lines are hidden by returning nil, which ends the hook chain:
    # only safe when ours is guaranteed to run last.
    return 'this Lich has no hook priorities.' unless hook_options(DownstreamHook).key?(:priority)
    return 'combat tracking is off. Turn it on with: ;e Lich::Gemstone::Combat::Tracker.enable!' unless combat::Tracker.enabled?

    nil
  end

  # The script's main loop in compress mode: feeds rounds and attack events
  # to the Compressor and prints what it releases.
  def self.run_compressor(queue, router)
    compressor = Compressor.new(->(events) { Summary.lines(events, hp: method(:hp_percent)) })
    loop do
      item = queue.pop(timeout: 0.25)
      now = Process.clock_gettime(Process::CLOCK_MONOTONIC)
      outputs = case item&.first
                when :round then compressor.add_round(item[1], item[2], item[3], now: now)
                when :event then compressor.add_event(item[1], now: now)
                else compressor.tick(now: now)
                end
      outputs.each { |lines| _respond(lines.map { |text| router.wrap(text) }.join) }
    rescue StandardError => e
      Lich.log "combatstream: compressor: #{e.class}: #{e.message}\n\t#{e.backtrace&.first(5)&.join("\n\t")}"
    end
  end

  # Estimated HP left, only for creatures with a real template: without one
  # Lich assumes a flat fallback max HP, and a percentage of that would
  # mislead.
  # @return [Integer, nil]
  def self.hp_percent(id)
    creature = Lich::Gemstone::Creature[id.to_i]
    return nil unless creature && !creature.dead? && creature.template&.max_hp.to_i.positive?

    creature.hp_percent&.round
  end
end

CombatStream.run(Script.current.vars[1..] || [])
